WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
“It is simple.” Why are they still lost?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Your words reveal only part of your map.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
Researchers studied the curse of knowledge in 1989.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Experiments compared different information states.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
What we know can bias what we predict.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
The expert says: press home.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
The newcomer sees a circle and a triangle.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
The sequence and signals remain unstated.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
They do not share the same starting point.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
This does not mean the newcomer is less capable.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
It does not mean all experts teach poorly.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
An information gap is one possible explanation.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Give a concrete action and a visible signal.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Invite a retelling to find a missing step.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Use retelling to check the explanation.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Start from what the other person can see.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Replace impatience with concrete detail.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Open the map. Then we can walk together.
